CH = dict(num=9, title="Subject-Verb Agreement", tag="Concord · Classes 5–8",
intro="A verb must agree with its subject in number (singular or plural) and person. This is called concord. Most errors in editing exercises arise from wrongly identifying the real subject, which may be hidden behind a phrase, a collective noun, or a joining word like 'or' or 'along with'. This chapter lists every rule you need.",
blocks=[
("h2","9.1 The Basic Rule"),
("note","NOTE","Grade guide: Classes 5–6: basic rule, is/are, has/have, don't/doesn't, there is/are, each/every. Classes 7–8: all rules 3–20 including nouns plural in form, either/or, as well as, titles, quantity, relative clauses and 'one of'."),
("p","A **singular subject** takes a **singular verb**; a **plural subject** takes a **plural verb**. In the present simple the singular verb ends in -s/-es (*he runs*); the plural does not (*they run*). For 'be': I **am**, he/she/it **is**, you/we/they **are**; past: I/he/she/it **was**, you/we/they **were**. For 'have': he/she/it **has**, others **have**; 'do/does'."),
("table",["Singular","Plural"],[["The boy **plays** football.","The boys **play** football."],["She **has** a pen.","They **have** pens."],["The dog **was** barking.","The dogs **were** barking."],["He **doesn't** know.","They **don't** know."]]),
("h2","9.2 Rules of Agreement"),
("h3","Rule 1 — Find the real subject (ignore the words between)"),
("p","A phrase between the subject and verb does not change the number. *The **box** of apples **is** heavy. The **quality** of these mangoes **is** poor. The **students** in the hall **are** waiting.*"),
("h3","Rule 2 — Two subjects joined by 'and' take a plural verb"),
("p","*Ram and Shyam **are** friends. Reading and writing **are** skills.*"),
("p","Exceptions — a singular verb when 'and' joins two names for **one person/thing** or a **single idea**: *The poet and philosopher **was** honoured.* (one person) *Bread and butter **is** my breakfast. Slow and steady **wins** the race. Rice and curry **is** a favourite dish.* With **each/every/no** before both nouns: *Every boy and girl **has** a book. No man and no woman **was** present.*"),
("h3","Rule 3 — Subjects joined by 'as well as', 'along with', 'together with', 'with', 'in addition to', 'besides', 'no less than', 'rather than'"),
("p","The verb agrees with the **first** subject: *The teacher, along with the students, **is** going. Rita, as well as her sisters, **was** present. The boys, together with their coach, **have** gone.*"),
("h3","Rule 4 — 'Or', 'nor', 'either…or', 'neither…nor'"),
("p","The verb agrees with the **nearer** subject: *Either the teacher or the students **are** wrong. Neither the students nor the teacher **is** present. Neither he nor I **am** guilty.* If possible place the plural subject nearer to the verb."),
("h3","Rule 5 — Each, every, either, neither, one, anyone, someone, everybody, nobody"),
("p","Always singular: *Each of the girls **has** a doll. Every student **was** present. Either of the two **is** correct. Neither of them **knows**. Everyone **is** here. Nobody **was** hurt. One of my friends **lives** in Pune.* After 'each' the noun is plural but the verb is singular: *Each of the boys* (plural boys) *has*."),
("h3","Rule 6 — Collective nouns"),
("p","If the group is considered as a **unit**, use a singular verb: *The team **is** winning. The committee **has** decided. The jury **is** united.* If members are thought of as **individuals**, use a plural verb: *The jury **were** divided in their opinions. The family **are** having their meals.* (Indian exam books prefer singular unless clearly individual.)"),
("h3","Rule 7 — Nouns plural in form but singular in meaning"),
("p","*News, mathematics, physics, economics, politics, ethics, measles, mumps, billiards, athletics, innings, gallows, Wales, the United States, the Philippines (as a country)* take a **singular** verb: *The news **is** true. Mathematics **is** my favourite subject. Measles **is** a disease.*"),
("h3","Rule 8 — Nouns always plural"),
("p","*Scissors, trousers, jeans, spectacles, pants, clothes, pliers, tongs, shorts, binoculars, goods, belongings, thanks, riches, savings, earnings, cattle, police, people* take a **plural** verb: *My trousers **are** torn. The police **have** arrived. Cattle **are** grazing.* But *a pair of trousers **is*** on the bed (the pair is singular)."),
("h3","Rule 9 — 'A number of' and 'the number of'"),
("p","*A number of students **are** absent* (= many; plural). *The number of students **is** small* (= the total; singular). Similarly: *a variety of* (plural), *the variety of* (singular); *a majority of* (plural), *the majority of the class* (usually plural)."),
("h3","Rule 10 — 'One of', 'each of', 'none of'"),
("p","*One of the boys **is** absent* (verb agrees with 'one'). *Each of the books **is** new.* *None of the boys **is/are** guilty* (singular is formal, plural is also accepted when it means 'not any'). *None of the milk **was** spilt* (uncountable, singular)."),
("h3","Rule 11 — Relative pronouns who, which, that"),
("p","The verb agrees with the **antecedent**: *He is one of the boys who **are** punctual.* (who → boys: plural). *He is the only one of the boys who **is** punctual.* (who → one). *I, who **am** your friend, will help you.* (who → I, so 'am')."),
("h3","Rule 12 — Amounts, distance, weight, time, money considered as a whole"),
("p","They take a **singular verb**: *Ten kilometres **is** a long distance. Fifty rupees **is** not enough. Two years **is** a short time. Five kilos **is** too heavy.* But when the individual units are meant: *Ten rupee notes **were** counted.* "),
("h3","Rule 13 — Fractions and 'of' phrases"),
("p","The verb agrees with the noun after 'of': *Half of the cake **is** left. Half of the cakes **are** left. Two-thirds of the land **is** barren. 30% of students **have** passed. A lot of money **is** wasted. A lot of people **are** waiting.* Same for *all of, some of, most of, the rest of, plenty of, a lot of, lots of*."),
("h3","Rule 14 — 'There is / There are' and 'Here is / Here are'"),
("p","The verb agrees with the noun that follows: *There **is** a book on the table. There **are** three books. Here **comes** the bus. There **was** a boy and a girl* (first noun; in careful writing, 'there were')."),
("h3","Rule 15 — Titles, names of books, newspapers, organisations"),
("p","Take a singular verb: *'Gulliver's Travels' **is** an interesting book. The Times of India **is** a newspaper. The Arabian Nights **is** a classic. The United Nations **was** founded in 1945. Tom and Jerry **is** a cartoon.*"),
("h3","Rule 16 — Sentences starting with 'what', 'who', 'whoever', 'whatever'"),
("p","Usually singular: *What you need **is** rest. Who **was** there? Whoever **comes** is welcome.* But *What are the facts?* if the following noun is plural."),
("h3","Rule 17 — Inverted sentences (verb before subject)"),
("p","Find the subject after the verb: *In the garden **grow** many flowers. Beyond the hills **lies** a village. Seldom **have** I seen such a sight.*"),
("h3","Rule 18 — The pronoun 'you'"),
("p","Always takes a plural verb: *You **are** my friend. You **were** right.* Even for one person."),
("h3","Rule 19 — Words like 'pair', 'dozen', 'series', 'means', 'species', 'headquarters'"),
("p","*A pair of shoes **is** missing. The means of transport **is** poor (a means = singular). This species **is** rare. The headquarters **is** in Delhi. A series of events **has** occurred. Two dozen eggs **are** needed.*"),
("h3","Rule 20 — Singular subject with 'and' + 'the' repeated"),
("p","If 'the' is used before each noun, they are two persons: *The secretary and the treasurer **are** coming* (two persons). *The secretary and treasurer **is** coming* (one person)."),
("h2","9.3 Subject-Verb Agreement in Tense Forms"),
("table",["Tense","Singular","Plural"],[
["Present simple","She plays.","They play."],["Present continuous","She is playing.","They are playing."],["Present perfect","She has played.","They have played."],["Past continuous","She was playing.","They were playing."],["Past perfect","She had played.","They had played."],["Future","She will play.","They will play."],["Passive","The song is sung.","The songs are sung."]]),
("note","NOTE","Past simple regular verbs and 'had', 'will', 'would', 'could', 'should', 'must' do not change with number: *He played / They played. She will come / They will come.* Only is/are, was/were, has/have, does/do (and -s verbs) change."),
("h2","9.4 Quick Reference Tables"),
("table",["Subject","Verb","Example"],[
["each, every, either, neither, one, anyone, everybody","singular","Everybody is happy."],
["news, mathematics, physics, economics, measles","singular","Physics is interesting."],
["scissors, trousers, police, cattle, people","plural","The police are here."],
["A and B (two things/persons)","plural","Ram and Mohan are twins."],
["A or B / neither A nor B","agree with nearer","Neither Ram nor his friends are going."],
["A as well as B / A along with B","agree with A","Ram as well as his friends is going."],
["a number of","plural","A number of boys are absent."],
["the number of","singular","The number of boys is large."],
["a lot of / some / all + uncountable","singular","All the water is dirty."],
["a lot of / some / all + plural","plural","All the books are old."],
["measurement, money, time as a whole","singular","Ten miles is far."]]),
("note","IMPORTANT","Common mistakes: (1) *The price of vegetables have gone up* → **has**. (2) *Each of the students have a book* → **has**. (3) *The news were shocking* → **was**. (4) *Neither of the answers are correct* → **is**. (5) *My brother, along with his friends, are coming* → **is**. (6) *Mathematics are difficult* → **is**. (7) *There is two chairs* → **are**. (8) *A number of reasons is given* → **are**. (9) *The scissors is blunt* → **are**. (10) *One of my friends are ill* → **is**."),
("qa",[("Choose: Each of the boys (has / have) a ticket.","has"),
("Choose: Neither Ram nor his brothers (is / are) present.","are (nearer subject: brothers)"),
("Choose: The teacher with his students (is / are) arriving.","is"),
("Choose: Fifty rupees (is / are) enough.","is"),
("Choose: A number of guests (has / have) come.","have"),
("Choose: He is one of those who (is / are) never late.","are")]),
],
takeaways=["The verb must agree with the real subject, not the nearest noun in a phrase.","Each, every, either, neither, everyone, nobody, and nouns like news and mathematics take a singular verb.","With or/nor use the nearer subject; with as well as/along with/with use the first subject.","'A number of' is plural; 'the number of' is singular.","In 'one of those who…', the verb is plural; in 'the only one who…', it is singular."])
